{"name":"Groovy Giraffe","hex":"#eeaa11","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"groovy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, pertaining to, or having grooves.","Set in one's ways."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Cool, neat, interesting. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Reminiscent of the counterculture of the 1960s; hippie, psychedelic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/groovy"},{"term":"giraffe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ruminant in the genus Giraffa of the African savannah with long legs, a highly elongated neck, yellow fur patterned with dark spots typically in the form of a network, and two or more horn-like projections called ossicones; the tallest living animal.","A giraffe unicycle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/giraffe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Groovy","blocks":[{"text":"Groovy is a slang colloquialism popular during the late 1950s to early 1970s. It is roughly synonymous with words such as \"excellent\", \"fashionable\", or \"amazing\", depending on context."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groovy","wikidata":"Q16840350"},{"title":"Giraffe","blocks":[{"text":"Giraffes are large African hoofed mammals. They are the tallest living terrestrial animals and the largest ruminants on Earth. They are classified under the family Giraffidae, along with their closest extant relative, the okapi. Traditionally, giraffes have been thought of as one species, Giraffa camelopardalis, with nine subspecies. Most recently, researchers proposed dividing them into four extant species, with seven subspecies, which can be distinguished morphologically by their fur coat patterns. Six valid extinct species of Giraffa are known from the fossil record."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Giraffe_Mikumi_National_Park.jpg/330px-Giraffe_Mikumi_National_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giraffe","wikidata":"Q862089"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Giraffa_camelopardis_distribution_2018.png/500px-Giraffa_camelopardis_distribution_2018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Giraffa_camelopardis_distribution_2018.png/500px-Giraffa_camelopardis_distribution_2018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGiraffa_camelopardis_distribution_2018.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Genetic_subdivision_in_the_giraffe_based_on_mitochondrial_DNA_sequences.png/500px-Genetic_subdivision_in_the_giraffe_based_on_mitochondrial_DNA_sequences.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Genetic_subdivision_in_the_giraffe_based_on_mitochondrial_DNA_sequences.png/500px-Genetic_subdivision_in_the_giraffe_based_on_mitochondrial_DNA_sequences.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map showing \"Approximate geographic ranges, fur patterns, and phylogenetic relationships between some giraffe subspecies based on mitochondrial DNA sequences. Coloured dots on the map represent sampling localities. The phylogenetic tree is a maximum-likelihood phylogram based on samples from 266 giraffes. Asterisks along branches correspond to node values of more than 90% bootstrap support. Stars at branch tips identify paraphyletic haplotypes found in Maasai and reticulated giraffes\".[6]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGenetic_subdivision_in_the_giraffe_based_on_mitochondrial_DNA_sequences.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Zoo_de_Vincennes%2C_Paris%2C_France_April_2014_%287%29%2C_crop.jpg/500px-Zoo_